      "body": "Setswana: Lekgetlo - plural dikgetlo\nEnglish: Fish scoop / Hand net\nIn history: \"The kitchen basket of the river\"\n\nFor people interested in culture and history, lekgetlo is the oldest fishing tool in Botswana. Before rods and nets, women and children in the Okavango, Chobe, and Limpopo used the lekgetlo to feed the family. It's simple, but it's genius.\n\n1. WHAT IS A LEKGETLO?\nMade by: Women. Weaving was a woman's skill passed from mother to daughter\nMaterials:\n- Frame: Flexible sticks from moretlwa or mokoba tree. Bent into a circle or oval\n- Net: Palm leaf strips lehlaka or reed lehlaka la noka. Woven tight\n- Handle: 1m - 2m long wooden pole tied to the frame\n\nShape: Like a big shallow basket on a stick. Some are round, some are D-shaped.\n\nHow it works: You push it through shallow water, grass, and reeds to scoop fish, tadpoles, and water insects.\n\n2. THE CULTURE + HISTORY BEHIND IT\nFor historians, lekgetlo shows 3 big things about life by the water:\n\nA. WOMEN'S WORK + FOOD SECURITY\nFishing with lekgetlo was women and children's work.\nWhen: Early morning and late afternoon when water is calm\nWhere: Shallow floodplains, edges of pans, and river banks\nWho ate it: Small fish dihlapi tse dinnye were dried go ebediwa and stored. Big protein source during dry season\n\nProverb: \"Mosadi yo o nang le lekgetlo ga a lale a tshwerwe ke tlala\" - \"A woman with a lekgetlo does not sleep hungry\"\n\nB. KNOWLEDGE OF WATER\nTo use lekgetlo well you must know:\n1.  Where fish hide: In grass, under lily pads, in mud\n2.  Seasons: After floods when fish are in shallow pools\n3.  Tides of the Delta: When water rises and falls\n\nElders could \"read\" the water by the ripples. The lekgetlo was an extension of their hand.\n\nC. COMMUNITY + TEACHING\n- Groups: Women went in groups of 5-10. Singing while scooping\n- Sharing: Catch was shared in the lapa. Nothing wasted\n- Teaching: Girls got their first small lekgetlo at age 6. Learn patience and skill\n\n3. HOW IT'S MADE - THE CRAFT\nTakes 1-2 days.\n\n1.  Cut sticks: Soak them so they bend without breaking\n2.  Weave: Over-under pattern with palm strips. Holes small enough to trap fish\n3.  Tie handle: With bark rope mogoga\n4.  Harden: Smoke it over fire so it lasts in water\n\nA good lekgetlo lasts 1-2 seasons. When it breaks, materials go back to the earth.\n\n4. LEKGETLO TODAY\nStill used: In Okavango villages like Seronga, Shakawe, and along the Chobe.\nWhy: Free, no license needed, and targets small fish without harming big fish.\n\n2 uses now:\n1.  Subsistence: Families still use it for food\n2.  Culture + Tourism: Demonstrated at Nhabe Museum and cultural villages for tourists\n\nRule: In the Delta, you can only use traditional tools like lekgetlo in community areas. Motors and big nets are for commercial fishing.\n\nModern version: Some use mosquito netting instead of reeds. Same shape.\n\n5. HOW TO EXPLAIN IT TO PEOPLE INTERESTED IN HISTORY:\n\"Lekgetlo is the traditional fish scoop of Botswana. It's a basket on a stick, woven by women from reeds and tree branches.\n\nFor hundreds of years, women in the Okavango and Chobe used it. They wade in shallow water and scoop. It catches small fish that you dry for the dry season.\n\nIt's not about sport. It's about survival and knowledge. To use it you must know where fish hide, how the river moves, and when to go.\n\nToday women still make them the same way. In museums you'll see them next to fish traps and drying racks. The lekgetlo shows how Batswana lived with the river, not against it.\"\n\nQuick facts:\n- Used by: Women and children\n- Target: Small fish, frogs, water bugs for bait\n- Best time: After floods, May - August\n- Regions: Okavango, Chobe, Limpopo, Boteti\n- Saying: \"Go kgela ka lekgetlo\" - \"To scoop with lekgetlo\" = to do something carefully and patiently\n\nSee it in real life:\n- Nhabe Museum, Maun: Full display of Bayei fishing tools including lekgetlo\n- Delta villages: Watch women use them at the river edge early morning\n- Phuthadikobo Museum, Mochudi: Has examples from Kgatleng rivers\n\nComparison of Traditional Fishing Tools:\n- Lekgetlo: Scoop net for shallow water. Women's tool\n- Mothopo: Fish trap made of reeds\n- Letholo: Spear for big fish. Men's tool\n- Mosako: Fishing line\n\n---\n\n(End of Topic)\n\n(Next Topic)",
      "key": "lekgetlo",
      "title": "Lekgetlo"
