      "body": "Location: D'kar Village, Ghanzi District, Western Botswana\nKuru = \"The people\" in Naro language\n\nFor people interested in culture and history, Kuru is the most important San museum in Botswana. It's not run by government. It's run by San people, for San people. The goal: \"Let us tell our own story.\"\n\n1. WHO ARE THE PEOPLE OF KURU?\nWho: Naro, G/ui, G//ana, and !Xoo San communities from the Kalahari\nWhere: D'kar is the heart of western Kalahari San land. Ghanzi is called \"The Capital of the Kalahari\"\nHistory: San people have lived in the Kalahari for 20,000+ years. They are the oldest continuous culture on earth. Kuru was started in 1990 because San history was being told by outsiders, or not told at all.\n\nKuru means \"the people\" in Naro. The name itself is a statement.\n\n2. THE MUSEUM: WHAT IT IS\nOpened: 1996\nRun by: Kuru Development Trust - a San-run NGO\nBuilding: Built in traditional style with thatch and mud, but with modern displays inside.\n\nWHAT'S INSIDE\n\nA. SAN HISTORY GALLERY\n- Stone Age to today: Tools 10,000 years old, next to photos of San people in the 1950s and today\n- The Relocation story: Honest exhibits about the 1990s-2000s when San were moved from Central Kalahari Game Reserve. This is told in their own words, with audio recordings.\n- Famous San: Photos and stories of San trackers, healers, and elders\n\nB. CULTURE GALLERY\nThis is the heart of it.\n- Hunting + Gathering: Real gemsbok bow and arrows, digging sticks, and snares. Video of how to find water in a tsama melon\n- Ostrich eggshell beads: Women still make these. You can see the whole process - takes 1 month for one necklace\n- Traditional clothes: Karo leather skirts, beadwork, and dancing costumes\n- Music: g//washi mouth bow, marimba, and recordings of trance dance singing. You can press play and hear it\n\nC. LIVING CULTURE ROOM\nNot old things behind glass. This shows San culture today.\n- Bush medicine: 50+ plants used for healing, with names in Naro and Setswana\n- Art: Contemporary San paintings telling stories of the Kalahari\n- Language: Recordings of Naro, G/ui, and !Xoo. San languages have the \"click\" sounds\n\nD. OUTSIDE\n- Traditional huts ntlo: 3 different styles from different San groups\n- Fire pit: Where storytelling happens\n- Craft shop: 100% San-owned. Baskets, beads, leatherwork, and paintings\n\n3. WHAT KURU CULTURE MEANS HISTORICALLY\nFor people studying history, Kuru shows 4 big things:\n\n1.  The oldest knowledge: San oral history goes back further than any written history. Stories about the stars, animals, and how the world began. The museum records these before elders pass.\n2.  Survival in the desert: San invented ways to find water, food like mongongo nuts and tsama melons, and medicine in the Kalahari. Anthropologists from all over the world study this.\n3.  Trance Dance n/um: This is spiritual + healing + history. Dancers enter trance to heal the sick and talk to ancestors. The museum has videos and explains the meaning of the paintings and songs.\n4.  Resilience: The exhibits don't hide hard history. They talk about discrimination, land loss, and poverty. But they also show San art, schools, and businesses today. It's \"we are still here.\"\n\nKuru's motto: \"Nothing about us, without us\"\n\n4. KURU BEYOND THE MUSEUM\nKuru is more than a museum. It's a whole cultural movement.\n\n- Kuru Art Project: San artists sell paintings worldwide. The dots and animals you see are ancient stories\n- Kuru Dance Group: Performs trance dance and tells stories at festivals\n- Radio: Kuru Family Radio broadcasts in Naro and other San languages\n- Literacy: Books written in San languages, because most San languages were never written down before\n\nEvery August there's the Kuru Dance Festival in D'kar. San from Namibia, Botswana, and SA come to dance for 3 days.\n\n5. VISITING TODAY\nLocation: D'kar Village, 15km from Ghanzi town on the A3. 650km from Gaborone.\nRun by: San board of directors + staff\nCost: Citizens P10, Visitors P30. Money goes to San programs.\nTime needed: 1.5 - 2 hours\nGuides: All guides are San. They'll show you how to make fire with sticks and tell you stories in English + Naro.\n\nRule: This is a living culture, not a zoo. Ask before taking photos of people.\n\nHOW TO EXPLAIN IT TO PEOPLE INTERESTED IN HISTORY:\n\"The Kuru Museum in D'kar is told by San people themselves. For 20,000 years San lived in the Kalahari as hunters and gatherers. They know every plant, every waterhole, and every star. Kuru collects their tools, their music, and their stories. But it also talks about today - how San are keeping language and dance alive. When you visit, a San elder might show you how to find water in a desert melon. That's history that's still alive.\"\n\nQuick facts:\n- Don't miss: The trance dance video and the ostrich-shell bead display\n- Best time: August for Kuru Dance Festival\n- Language: You'll hear click sounds. Naro has 4 different clicks\n- Food: Try phane and mongongo nuts sold at the craft shop\n\nComparison of Botswana Culture Museums:\n- National Museum Gaborone: Whole country overview\n- Supa Ngwao Francistown: Kalanga culture\n- Khama III Serowe: Bangwato chiefs\n- Kuru D'kar: San culture, told by San people\n\n---",
