      "body": "Location: Ntsweng Village, 15km west of Gaborone, South-East District\nAlso called: Ntsweng Hill / \"Old Gaborone\"\n\nFor people interested in culture and history, Ntsweng is where modern Gaborone began. It's not a building with glass cases. It's an open-air heritage site + small museum that tells the story of Kgosi Gaborone and the founding of the Bakwena capital.\n\n1. WHO WAS KGOSI GABORONE & WHAT IS NTSWENG?\nTime: 1880s - 1890s\nWho: Kgosi Gaborone of the Bakwena tribe. Gaborone city is named after him.\nWhat was Ntsweng: This hill was the capital of the Bakwena from about 1880 to 1890. It had over 10,000 people.\n\nWhy they left: In 1890 there was a drought and fighting. Kgosi Gaborone moved the people 15km east to where Gaborone city is today, next to the Notwane River. That became \"Gaberon\" and later \"Gaborone\".\n\nSo Ntsweng = \"Old Gaborone\". The city you're in now started here.\n\n2. THE HERITAGE SITE + MUSEUM\nOpened as heritage site: 1990s\nRun by: National Museum of Botswana + Bakwena Tribal Administration\n\nIt's 2 parts:\n\nA. THE OPEN-AIR SITE ON THE HILL\nYou walk the actual village. Nothing is reconstructed, so you see real history.\n- Stone wall foundations: Remains of 200+ huts ntlo in circles. You can see the family compounds\n- Cattle kraals losaka: Huge stone circles where Bakwena kept thousands of cattle. Cattle = wealth\n- Grain pits: Underground storage pits for sorghum mabele\n- Kgotla area: The flat area where Kgosi Gaborone held court\n\nGuides will show you how the village was laid out: Chief's area on top, then elders, then everyone else.\n\nB. THE SMALL INTERPRETATION CENTRE / MUSEUM\nAt the base of the hill.\n- Photos: 1890s photos of Ntsweng taken by missionaries\n- Artifacts found here: Pottery, grinding stones diphoko, iron tools, and beads\n- Bakwena history board: Family tree: Kgosi Gaborone → Kgosi Sebele I → Kgosi Bathoen → Sir Seretse Khama\n- Maps: Shows how people moved from Ntsweng → Gaborone Village → Gaborone City in 1966\n\n3. WHAT IT MEANS CULTURALLY / HISTORICALLY\nFor historians, Ntsweng matters for 3 big reasons:\n\n1.  The origin of Gaborone: Every time you say \"Gaborone\" you're saying Kgosi Gaborone's name. This is where his village was. It connects the capital city to a person and a hill.\n2.  Typical 1800s Tswana town: Archaeologists study Ntsweng to understand how big Tswana capitals worked before cars and shops. The stone walls, cattle by number, and kgotla layout are all still used today.\n3.  Resilience + Movement: Batswana moved capitals often for water, grass, and safety. Ntsweng → Gaborone is one example. The museum explains this \"moving capital\" culture.\n\nElders say: \"Fa o sa itse kwa o tswang, o ka se itse kwa o yang\" - \"If you don't know where you come from, you won't know where you're going.\"\n\n4. VISITING TODAY\nLocation: 15km from Gaborone CBD, off the A1 to Lobatse. Turn at Kgale.\nCost: Citizens P5, Visitors P20\nTime needed: 1.5 - 2 hours. Wear walking shoes - it's a hill climb.\nGuides: Bakwena guides from nearby villages. They'll tell you oral history about Kgosi Gaborone.\n\nBest time: May - September. It's hot and open, no shade.\nWhat to bring: Water, hat. There are no shops on the hill.\n\nThere are school trips here every year. Kids learn that their capital has roots.\n\nHOW TO EXPLAIN IT TO PEOPLE INTERESTED IN HISTORY:\n\"Ntsweng Heritage Museum is 'Old Gaborone'. In the 1880s this hill was the capital of Kgosi Gaborone and the Bakwena. There were 10,000 people living in stone-walled huts here. In 1890 they moved down to the river and that became Gaborone city. The site has the real foundations of huts and cattle kraals. The small museum shows photos and artifacts. If you want to understand where Gaborone came from, you have to start at Ntsweng.\"\n\nQuick facts:\n- Don't miss: Stand on top of the hill and look toward Gaborone city. You'll see why they moved - better water\n- Connection: Kgosi Gaborone → Sir Seretse Khama. Same royal line\n- Nearby: 20min from Gaborone. Easy half-day trip\n\nComparison:\n- National Museum Gaborone: National story\n- Kgosi Sechele Museum Molepolole: Bakwena history + Livingstone\n- Ntsweng: The first Bakwena capital near Gaborone\n- Phuthadikobo Mochudi: Bakgatla capital\n\n-------------------",
      "key": "ntsweng heritage museum",
      "title": "Ntsweng Heritage Museum"
